WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.556
Welcome to Module 3 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.556 --> 00:00:06.763
make joiners ready on day one.

3
00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:12.151
This is Gate 3, Joiners, with
Chapter 03 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:12.151 --> 00:00:15.476
Modules 1 and 2 settled who
writes each account state,

5
00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:18.575
and brought contractors
in from their own source.

6
00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:20.537
Now the new hire's first morning.

7
00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:25.321
In this module you'll keep the Lifecycle
Workflows definitions as reviewed files,

8
00:00:25.321 --> 00:00:29.541
lint them before they reach the tenant,
plan each change against what the

9
00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:34.113
tenant already holds, and measure how many
hires were actually ready on day one.

10
00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:37.123
You'll also see why the
Temporary Access Pass a new

11
00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:39.987
hire gets should be
short-lived and one-time use.

12
00:00:39.987 --> 00:00:43.400
The code runs on your laptop
against fictional fixtures.

13
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.070
It's illustrative: unit-tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.820
Why does the first morning need code?

15
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.470
The pipeline sends a new hire's
account early, and disabled.

16
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.880
On the first morning, that person
needs an enabled account, a license,

17
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.132
baseline access, and a way to sign
in before their PIV card is bound.

18
00:01:07.132 --> 00:01:09.633
Lifecycle Workflows can do all of that.

19
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:10.945
But here's the catch.

20
00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:14.608
Each workflow is a definition that
someone can change in the portal.

21
00:01:14.608 --> 00:01:19.295
And Learn makes every change a new
version, rather than an in-place update.

22
00:01:19.295 --> 00:01:22.970
So in this module you keep the
workflows as reviewed files.

23
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.220
You lint them before
they reach the tenant.

24
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.245
You plan each change against
what the tenant holds.

25
00:01:28.245 --> 00:01:32.144
And then you measure how many hires
were actually ready on day one,

26
00:01:32.144 --> 00:01:36.320
because a workflow that ran isn't
the same as a person who can work.

27
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.881
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:42.881 --> 00:01:45.245
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:45.245 --> 00:01:48.450
You'll explain why workflows
belong in reviewed files,

30
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:53.008
and how the plan decides between creating
a workflow and making a new version.

31
00:01:53.008 --> 00:01:56.123
You'll run the workflow
lint and explain its

32
00:01:56.123 --> 00:01:59.758
Temporary Access Pass
findings, the J W oh five rule.

33
00:01:59.758 --> 00:02:02.808
You'll explain why the workflow
budget is held to fifty.

34
00:02:02.808 --> 00:02:06.858
And you'll measure the day-one readiness
rate, and say what makes a hire ready.

35
00:02:06.858 --> 00:02:08.595
Two modules come first.

36
00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:12.655
From Module 1: in this program,
the pipeline's active value,

37
00:02:12.655 --> 00:02:16.370
not the day-one workflow,
enables the account at hire.

38
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.333
From Module 2: the contractor leaver
workflow, which the files include.

39
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.606
Lab 2 needs a worldwide
or G C C test tenant

40
00:02:24.606 --> 00:02:28.820
with Entra ID Governance
trial or test licenses.

41
00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.870
Read the guide's Step 3,
and notice three things.

42
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.795
First, the workflow budget.

43
00:02:37.795 --> 00:02:42.292
Learn gives two tenant ceilings:
fifty workflows on its licensing page,

44
00:02:42.292 --> 00:02:44.808
and a hundred on its service limits page.

45
00:02:44.808 --> 00:02:50.157
The guide budgets to fifty until Microsoft
reconciles them; that's design guidance.

46
00:02:50.157 --> 00:02:52.744
Second, the Temporary Access Pass.

47
00:02:52.744 --> 00:02:56.082
The tenant allows a pass from
ten minutes to thirty days,

48
00:02:56.082 --> 00:03:00.508
but Learn recommends at most twenty-four
hours for a pass sent to a new hire.

49
00:03:00.508 --> 00:03:03.797
Third, baseline access
comes by access package,

50
00:03:03.797 --> 00:03:06.833
never by adding the user
to a group directly.

51
00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:12.424
Then read the design in Chapter 08 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook's HR-Driven

52
00:03:12.424 --> 00:03:17.457
Identity book: its guardrails at agency
scale, and its triggers and schedule.

53
00:03:17.457 --> 00:03:20.580
And keep Module 1's rule
in mind: in this program,

54
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.720
the pipeline's active value enables
the account at hire, not a workflow.

55
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:33.188
Here are the six workflow files, as
reviewed version one point oh bodies.

56
00:03:33.188 --> 00:03:34.438
Two are joiners.

57
00:03:34.438 --> 00:03:37.965
The pre-hire workflow runs
seven days before the hire date

58
00:03:37.965 --> 00:03:41.713
and generates the Temporary Access
Pass and sends it by email.

59
00:03:41.713 --> 00:03:44.305
The day-one workflow runs on the hire date

60
00:03:44.305 --> 00:03:47.125
and requests the
onboarding access package.

61
00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:50.227
One is the mover, triggered
by an attribute change:

62
00:03:50.227 --> 00:03:54.388
it notifies the manager and removes
an access package assignment.

63
00:03:54.388 --> 00:03:55.949
And three are leavers.

64
00:03:55.949 --> 00:04:00.814
On the leave date, employees are disabled,
their refresh tokens revoked and their

65
00:04:00.814 --> 00:04:05.750
package assignments removed; contractors
are disabled and their tokens revoked.

66
00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.761
Thirty days after the leave date,

67
00:04:07.761 --> 00:04:11.112
a separate post-separation
workflow deletes the user.

68
00:04:11.112 --> 00:04:12.400
Notice the scopes.

69
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.501
Every one reads the worker type
and a coded slot, never a name,

70
00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:20.710
so renaming an organization
can't silently drop anyone.

71
00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.978
The lint, rules J W oh one to fifteen,

72
00:04:26.978 --> 00:04:30.935
checks the files before
anything reaches the tenant.

73
00:04:30.935 --> 00:04:31.910
A few to know.

74
00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.730
J W oh two warns when a scope
uses no coded attribute,

75
00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.822
or reads a display value like department.

76
00:04:38.822 --> 00:04:44.238
J W oh three and oh six catch an offset
beyond a hundred eighty days either way,

77
00:04:44.238 --> 00:04:47.935
and more than Learn's
twenty-five tasks in a workflow.

78
00:04:47.935 --> 00:04:53.833
J W oh five is the pass: a lifetime outside
ten minutes to thirty days is a finding,

79
00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:58.097
and over twenty-four hours, or
not one-time use, is a warning.

80
00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.698
J W oh seven is over budget.

81
00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:06.198
J W oh eight is a scheduled workflow
with no execution limit recorded; Learn

82
00:05:06.198 --> 00:05:11.610
says the limit isn't in the API, so you
set it in the admin center and record it.

83
00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.472
J W oh nine is a direct group add.

84
00:05:14.472 --> 00:05:17.735
J W eleven is delete and disable together.

85
00:05:17.735 --> 00:05:21.450
Some rules come from Learn,
and some are design guidance.

86
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.388
Two more ideas.

87
00:05:26.388 --> 00:05:30.346
The plan compares the files with an
export of the tenant's workflows,

88
00:05:30.346 --> 00:05:32.113
matching them by display name.

89
00:05:32.113 --> 00:05:34.563
A file with no match becomes a create.

90
00:05:34.563 --> 00:05:37.832
A file that changed becomes a
body for create new version,

91
00:05:37.832 --> 00:05:40.962
because Learn makes a definition
change a new version.

92
00:05:40.962 --> 00:05:43.462
An unchanged file is left alone.

93
00:05:43.462 --> 00:05:48.600
A workflow in the tenant that no file
defines gets the J W fifteen warning.

94
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.538
And the plan refuses to run on
files that still have lint findings.

95
00:05:52.538 --> 00:05:53.674
Then readiness.

96
00:05:53.674 --> 00:05:58.592
A hire is ready on day one only with
all four: an account that exists and is

97
00:05:58.592 --> 00:06:04.175
enabled, a license, the onboarding package
delivered, and a completed pre-hire run.

98
00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:08.453
The readiness rate is ready hires
over hires for each start date,

99
00:06:08.453 --> 00:06:12.130
and it's saved in a summary
file that Module 7 reads.

100
00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:16.605
Try it.

101
00:06:16.605 --> 00:06:22.167
Lab 1 runs in the hr-lifecycle folder,
and nothing signs in or sends a request.

102
00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:24.730
Step one lists two tasks' reads.

103
00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:26.990
The plan needs only one permission,

104
00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:31.355
the Lifecycle Workflows workflow
read, for one GET on the workflows.

105
00:06:31.355 --> 00:06:35.596
Readiness needs four permissions,
for three GET calls: the users,

106
00:06:35.596 --> 00:06:40.718
each workflow's user processing results,
and the access package assignments.

107
00:06:40.718 --> 00:06:45.579
Step two lints the program's own six
workflow files with a budget of fifty.

108
00:06:45.579 --> 00:06:49.730
Zero findings, zero warnings,
zero information items.

109
00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:54.413
That's the clean run an ISSO keeps:
it shows a one-time pass of at most

110
00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:59.810
twenty-four hours, and an execution
limit on every scheduled workflow.

111
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.533
Step three lints three broken copies,

112
00:07:06.533 --> 00:07:09.985
with a budget of two so
you can see J W oh seven.

113
00:07:09.985 --> 00:07:13.722
Seven findings and four warnings,
and an exit code of two.

114
00:07:13.722 --> 00:07:14.610
Read a few.

115
00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:17.873
One offset is two hundred
days, beyond the limit.

116
00:07:17.873 --> 00:07:20.073
A leaver is triggered on the hire date.

117
00:07:20.073 --> 00:07:22.650
Three workflows are
over the budget of two,

118
00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.522
and the message quotes
both of Learn's ceilings.

119
00:07:25.522 --> 00:07:29.072
A scheduled workflow has no
execution limit recorded.

120
00:07:29.072 --> 00:07:31.764
A joiner adds the user to groups directly,

121
00:07:31.764 --> 00:07:34.772
instead of giving baseline
access by package.

122
00:07:34.772 --> 00:07:36.422
The slide trims a few more.

123
00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:41.923
Now find the two J W oh five warnings,
the ones this module cares most about.

124
00:07:41.923 --> 00:07:46.486
One pass lasts four thousand three hundred
twenty minutes, which is three days,

125
00:07:46.486 --> 00:07:50.136
well over the twenty-four hours
Learn recommends for a new hire.

126
00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.790
The other pass isn't one-time use.

127
00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:01.440
Step four plans the program's files against
an export of the tenant's workflows.

128
00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.728
The day-one workflow changed,
so it gets a new version.

129
00:08:04.728 --> 00:08:09.066
The employee last-day leaver isn't
in the tenant yet, so it's a create.

130
00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:11.703
The other four are
unchanged and left alone.

131
00:08:11.703 --> 00:08:16.666
There's one warning, J W fifteen, for
a workflow called Onboard Marketing,

132
00:08:16.666 --> 00:08:20.703
legacy: someone made it in the
portal, and no file defines it.

133
00:08:20.703 --> 00:08:24.027
The plan is written to a
file, and nothing is sent.

134
00:08:24.027 --> 00:08:26.090
Now try planning the broken folder.

135
00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:30.803
It stops with an error: the files have
lint findings, fix them before planning.

136
00:08:30.803 --> 00:08:32.514
So the order is enforced.

137
00:08:32.514 --> 00:08:36.320
Lint first, then plan,
then a reviewed send.

138
00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.957
Step five measures day one.

139
00:08:41.957 --> 00:08:46.944
You give it the HR hires, the hire
accounts, the pre-hire workflow's results,

140
00:08:46.944 --> 00:08:51.930
the access package assignments, the
workflow files, a moment, eight in the

141
00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:56.583
morning U T C on the second of November,
the cloud, and an output folder.

142
00:08:56.583 --> 00:08:59.694
One of four hires was
ready: twenty-five percent.

143
00:08:59.694 --> 00:09:01.982
Three hires get a readiness warning,

144
00:09:01.982 --> 00:09:06.633
because each one is missing at least
one of the four things a ready hire has.

145
00:09:06.633 --> 00:09:10.203
The summary file holds the
rate, zero point two five,

146
00:09:10.203 --> 00:09:13.858
and Module 7 reads it as one
of the lifecycle metrics.

147
00:09:13.858 --> 00:09:16.641
Step six runs the tests, fifty-seven pass,

148
00:09:16.641 --> 00:09:19.940
and you delete the
chapter's output folder.

149
00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:29.711
Lab 2 runs one change in your own test
tenant: worldwide or G C C, never production,

150
00:09:29.711 --> 00:09:33.416
with Entra ID Governance
trial or test licenses,

151
00:09:33.416 --> 00:09:37.028
and test users whose hire
date is a few days away.

152
00:09:37.028 --> 00:09:39.953
Export the tenant's
workflows, plan one change,

153
00:09:39.953 --> 00:09:44.727
and send it with Graph PowerShell as
a Lifecycle Workflows Administrator.

154
00:09:44.727 --> 00:09:49.207
Set the execution limit in the admin
center, since it isn't in the API.

155
00:09:49.207 --> 00:09:51.977
Check that the manager receives the pass.

156
00:09:51.977 --> 00:09:54.427
And on the start date, measure readiness.

157
00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:58.628
The reader app needs a certificate
and one permission, the workflow read.

158
00:09:58.628 --> 00:10:02.933
To back it out, delete a workflow the
lab created, which can be restored

159
00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:07.164
for thirty days, or send the previous
file's body as a further version.

160
00:10:07.164 --> 00:10:10.912
Then remove the reader app,
its permission and certificate,

161
00:10:10.912 --> 00:10:12.977
and delete the export and output.

162
00:10:12.977 --> 00:10:15.490
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

163
00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.627
The federal requirement.

164
00:10:20.627 --> 00:10:25.450
This gate meets NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

165
00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:29.028
enhancement one, automated
system account management.

166
00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:33.719
It meets I A five: the initial
authenticator is time-limited, and goes to

167
00:10:33.719 --> 00:10:38.491
an accountable person; that's the short,
one-time pass sent to the manager.

168
00:10:38.491 --> 00:10:44.264
And it meets O M B M-22-09's
phishing-resistant multifactor authentication,

169
00:10:44.264 --> 00:10:48.915
which the Temporary Access Pass
bridges to until the PIV card is bound.

170
00:10:48.915 --> 00:10:53.800
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate
3: the reviewed workflow files,

171
00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:58.052
and the plan that changed each
version, with its manifest.

172
00:10:58.052 --> 00:11:01.426
The lint's clean run, showing
a one-time pass of at most

173
00:11:01.426 --> 00:11:05.977
twenty-four hours and an execution
limit on every scheduled workflow.

174
00:11:05.977 --> 00:11:08.465
The readiness summary for each start date.

175
00:11:08.465 --> 00:11:13.470
And the workflows' run history,
exported past Entra's thirty-day view.

176
00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.195
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:19.195 --> 00:11:23.195
First: you change the pre-hire
workflow's offset in its file.

178
00:11:23.195 --> 00:11:24.920
What does the plan produce for it?

179
00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.781
The answer is a: a body
for create new version,

180
00:11:27.781 --> 00:11:31.358
because Learn makes a definition
change a new version.

181
00:11:31.358 --> 00:11:33.096
There's no in-place update.

182
00:11:33.096 --> 00:11:36.320
A file with no match in the
tenant would become a create.

183
00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.370
Second: which hire counts
as ready on day one?

184
00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:44.082
The answer is c: one whose account
exists and is enabled, holds a license,

185
00:11:44.082 --> 00:11:48.795
has the onboarding package delivered,
and has a completed pre-hire run.

186
00:11:48.795 --> 00:11:49.758
All four.

187
00:11:49.758 --> 00:11:52.876
An account that exists but
is disabled isn't ready,

188
00:11:52.876 --> 00:11:55.919
and neither is a pre-hire
run that didn't complete.

189
00:11:55.919 --> 00:12:01.170
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

190
00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:05.745
To sum up.

191
00:12:05.745 --> 00:12:10.008
You linted the program's six workflow
files and three broken ones.

192
00:12:10.008 --> 00:12:13.595
You planned a create and a new
version against a tenant export,

193
00:12:13.595 --> 00:12:15.846
and saw a portal-made workflow flagged.

194
00:12:15.846 --> 00:12:19.420
And you measured a day-one readiness
rate of twenty-five percent.

195
00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:24.209
Carry one thing forward: the day-one
workflow requests the onboarding package,

196
00:12:24.209 --> 00:12:27.082
but department access
comes from the next gate.

197
00:12:27.082 --> 00:12:32.507
Next is Module 4: assign access from
attributes and review what a move leaves.

198
00:12:32.507 --> 00:12:36.812
You'll lint access package assignment
policies before they reach the tenant,

199
00:12:36.812 --> 00:12:39.772
scan the tenant's policies
for the member-of operator,

200
00:12:39.772 --> 00:12:43.680
and list what a move leaves behind
for the manager of record to review.
